[1] In the 1930s, much of eastern Kentucky had no public libraries, few paved roads, and {{1|its literacy rates were among the lowest}} in the nation. [2] In 1935, the Works Progress Administration, a federal agency created to provide jobs during the Great Depression, {{2|beginning to fund}} a program to bring books to these isolated communities. [3] The workers it hired, most of them local women, became known as pack horse librarians. {{3|}}

[4] Each librarian rode a horse or mule along a regular route, following creek beds and steep {{4|mountain trails that went up and down the mountains}}. [5] {{5|In winter, the trails were often icy; in spring, they turned to deep mud.}} [6] {{6|Still,}} the librarians rarely missed a scheduled visit. [7] Families eagerly awaited the deliveries, and children often ran down the hollows to meet the riders {{7|on its}} way up.

[8] The program owned few books of its own. [9] Most of the reading material {{8|was donated, by}} libraries, schools, and private citizens from across the country. [10] When magazines and books wore out, the librarians cut out usable pages and pasted them into scrapbooks {{9|of various things}}. [11] These homemade collections {{10|were a huge hit with the folks}} along the routes.

[12] The program ended in 1943, when the demands of World War II {{11|was drawing}} federal money and workers to other purposes. [13] By then, the pack horse librarians had carried books and magazines to many thousands of readers. [14] {{12|The horses used in the program were sturdy animals well suited to rough ground.}}

[1] During World War II, the U.S. Forest Service worried that wildfires would destroy timber that the nation needed for the war effort. [2] In 1944, working with the Ad Council, the agency introduced a new spokesperson for fire prevention: a bear wearing {{1|jeans, and a campaign hat}}. [3] The first poster showed the bear pouring a bucket of water on a campfire. [4] In 1947, the campaign adopted the slogan "Only you can prevent forest fires."

[5] {{2|Bears are found in many national forests.}} [6] That spring, firefighters battling a blaze in the Capitan Mountains of New Mexico found a badly burned bear cub clinging to a charred tree. {{3|}} [7] The cub, {{4|who}} officials later named Smokey, was treated for his burns and sent to the National Zoo in Washington, D.C., where he {{5|lives}} until 1976.

[8] The real Smokey quickly became one of the most recognizable animals in the country, {{6|and many people knew about him}}. [9] In 2001, the campaign {{7|updated and revised}} its slogan to "Only you can prevent wildfires." [10] {{8|Smokey Bear remains a popular figure today.}}
